# ============================ POS 7 ============================
LESSONS_M7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The voucher programme and the console", "10", "<p>Module 2 configured the voucher settings on the profile; module 4 covered selling a voucher at the till. This module covers running the programme itself, using the <b>Voucher Console</b> — the full-screen workspace, reached at the site's /vouchers address, that keeps a complete record of every card from creation to final redemption. The module's law: <b>a voucher is money the business owes, tracked from creation to expiry.</b></p><p><b>What a voucher is.</b> A voucher is a promise of value: a card worth 5,000 can be spent by whoever holds it, in your stores. From the moment a card is sold, that value is money the business owes — and it stays owed until the card is spent or expires. Everything in this module follows from that: the accounting rules (Chapter 3), the activation control on free cards (Chapter 2), the printing rules (Chapter 5), and the checks (Chapter 8).</p><p><b>The roles.</b> Access to the console is role-based:</p><p>• <b>Voucher Manager</b> — everything in the console: create, import, activate, print, browse and cancel cards, and edit the Setup and Design screens. At Okelewo this is the accountant.<br>• <b>Administrator</b> — everything, plus accounts, roles and system-wide settings.<br>• <b>Accounts Manager</b> — the accounts the programme posts to, and the recognition of lapsed value as income (Chapter 7).<br>• <b>Counter staff</b> — sell and redeem at the till with ordinary till permissions. They work in the till, not the console.</p><p>Note the separation: the person who <b>creates</b> voucher value, the people who <b>handle</b> it at counters, and the person who <b>accounts</b> for it are different capabilities, assigned separately. Keep them separate — Chapter 8 explains what goes wrong when one person holds all three.</p><p><b>The console's sections.</b> The left-hand rail:</p><p>• <b>Overview</b> — the whole programme at a glance (Chapter 6).<br>• <b>Batches</b> — every batch of cards; open one to activate or print.<br>• <b>Vouchers</b> — the searchable register of every individual card.<br>• <b>Generate</b> — create a new batch.<br>• <b>Import</b> — bring pre-printed card stock into the system.<br>• <b>Design</b> — the printed card's appearance, per company.<br>• <b>Setup</b> — the accounts the programme posts to, per company.</p><p>Setup and Design are configured once per company and rarely touched again. The other five are the everyday programme. The module covers them in the order value moves: accounts first (Chapter 3 — nothing should be created before the books are wired), then design and creation (4), activation and printing (5), the counter and the register (6), the accounting and the monthly check (7), and what to watch (8).</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Two kinds of card, six states, one gate", "12", "<p><b>The two kinds.</b> Every card is one of:</p><p>• <b>Sellable</b> — a gift card a customer buys. Its validity clock starts at the <b>sale</b>: valid for the configured number of days from the day it is bought. Sellable cards are created ready to sell.<br>• <b>Complimentary</b> — a card given away free: a prize, an apology, a promotion. Complimentary cards are created <b>inactive</b> and cannot be used until a manager <b>activates</b> them. Their validity clock starts at <b>activation</b>.</p><p><b>Why the activation gate exists.</b> No money changes hands when complimentary cards are created, so a batch of them is, until activated, a box of potential money that cost nothing to make. The gate means: <b>a stolen or prematurely released batch cannot be redeemed</b> — the cards are worthless until someone with authority switches them on. Creating cards (generating or importing) therefore never creates spendable value by itself; a second, recorded act by an authorised person does. Assign that authority deliberately — it belongs to the Voucher Manager, not to whoever happens to print.</p><p><b>The six states.</b> Every card is always in exactly one state:</p><p>• <b>Available</b> — live stock: a sellable card ready to sell, or a complimentary card not yet activated.<br>• <b>Sold</b> — issued and carrying its full value (bought at a till, or activated as complimentary).<br>• <b>Partially redeemed</b> — some value spent; a balance remains.<br>• <b>Redeemed</b> — fully spent.<br>• <b>Expired</b> — the validity period ended with value still on the card (Chapter 7 covers what happens to that value).<br>• <b>Void</b> — cancelled by a manager. <b>A void card can never be redeemed.</b> Cancellation is permanent; a card voided in error is replaced by creating or importing another, and the cancellation itself stays on the record.</p><p><b>Two constants per card.</b> Whatever the state:</p><p>• The <b>balance</b> — starts at face value, falls as the card is spent.<br>• The <b>serial number</b> — the card's permanent reference, printed on the card and shown in the console. Every search, dispute and history lookup resolves to it.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Sellable cards: ready at creation, clock from sale. Complimentary cards: inactive at creation, activated by a manager, clock from activation. Six states from Available to Void; a void is final and stays on record; the balance and serial identify every card for life. Chapter 3 wires the accounts these cards will post to; Chapter 5 operates the activation gate described here.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Setup: the accounts", "12", "<p>Because a sold voucher is money owed, the sale has to post to the right accounts. The Setup screen configures this once per company — <b>before the first card is created</b>.</p><p><b>The two accounts.</b></p><p>• <b>Liability account.</b> When a customer buys a gift card, the cash comes in and this account records what the business now owes. It must be a liability-type account belonging to the company. Every voucher sale credits it.<br>• <b>Promotional expense account</b> (optional). Where the cost of complimentary cards is recorded when they are redeemed. If left blank, complimentary redemptions fall back to the liability account — it works, but the books are clearer with the expense account named.</p><p><b>Two ways to complete Setup.</b></p><p>1. <b>Choose your own accounts</b> from the company's chart. The console validates each choice on four points: it belongs to the right company, it is the right <b>kind</b> of account, it is a real <b>posting</b> account (not a group heading), and it is <b>active</b>. Pass all four and the console wires the rest automatically.<br>2. <b>Create defaults for me.</b> One click creates a suitable liability account, an expense account, <b>the voucher product, its tax treatment, and the till payment method</b> — everything listed on screen for later adjustment.</p><p>Either way, a status banner then confirms the company is <b>ready to issue vouchers</b> and names its liability account. (A company that never opens Setup still works on system-standard accounts — Setup makes the books readable, it is not what makes vouchers function.)</p><p><b>The accounting rule behind it.</b> Understand this once and Chapter 7 is easy: <b>selling a voucher is not a sale — it is a promise.</b> The customer has paid, but no goods have been handed over. So:</p><p>• The money received is recorded as a <b>liability</b> — value owed — never as revenue.<br>• <b>No sales tax is charged</b> on the voucher sale. Tax belongs on the goods, and the goods have not been bought yet.<br>• Revenue and tax are recognised <b>later, at redemption</b>, on the goods the customer actually takes.</p><p><b>Why getting this wrong matters.</b> A business that books voucher sales as revenue makes four errors at once: the selling month is overstated, the redemption month is understated, tax is charged at the wrong time, and the outstanding-liability figure — what the business actually owes cardholders — disappears from the books. A strong December voucher push raises <b>cash</b> and raises <b>debt</b> in equal measure; the revenue arrives in January when the cards come back. Okelewo's MD summarised it correctly in Chapter 8: a voucher programme is a loan from customers who intend to spend it with you.</p><p><b>Order of operations.</b> Setup first, then Design, then the first batch. Nothing is minted into unwired books.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Design, Generate, and Import", "13", "<p><b>Design.</b> Every printed card has two halves: the <b>left half</b> is the branded panel — logo, colours and wording, configured per company — and the <b>right half</b> is a standard information panel identical on every card: usage notes, the barcode, the written code, the serial number and the validity. Two ways to design the branded half:</p><p>• <b>Guided design.</b> Upload a logo (optionally a strip of feature icons), pick background, highlight and text colours, write two short taglines (one for gift cards, one for complimentary), and add footer lines — website, email, social. The console lays it out.<br>• <b>Custom design.</b> Supply your own artwork and layout; the console inserts each card's live details — amount, company name, the correct tagline, footers — so one design serves every value and both kinds.</p><p>Two useful facts: the <b>live preview is drawn by the same engine that does the real printing</b>, so what you see is genuinely what prints; and a company with <b>no saved design</b> prints on a clean neutral panel built from its name — you can start selling immediately and design later.</p><p><b>Generate — creating a new batch.</b> Five choices:</p><p>1. <b>Type</b> — Complimentary (created inactive) or Sellable (created ready).<br>2. <b>Company</b>.<br>3. <b>Value</b> — a preset or any typed amount. <b>Every card in a batch carries the same value</b>; a batch is one denomination, which keeps counting and reporting simple.<br>4. <b>Quantity</b> — up to <b>10,000</b> cards per batch.<br>5. <b>Validity</b> — days usable, counted from sale (sellable) or activation (complimentary).</p><p>Press Generate: the console creates the batch and assigns every card a unique code and serial number, then opens the batch — sellable batches ready to print and sell, complimentary batches waiting for activation.</p><p><b>Import — bringing pre-printed stock in.</b> For physical cards that already exist (for example older stock with printed serials):</p><p>1. Enter company, value and validity, as with Generate.<br>2. Enter the serials: pasted one per line, comma-separated, or as a range — <b>0001-0500</b> imports five hundred. The screen confirms how many it found.<br>3. Review the preview and confirm.</p><p>Two rules about imported cards:</p><p>• <b>The number already printed on each card stays the code used to redeem it.</b> Cards in circulation keep working unchanged.<br>• Imported cards arrive as an <b>inactive complimentary batch</b> and must be activated like any other. Legacy stock is a box of potential money exactly like a fresh batch, so the same gate applies.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Design once per company (guided or custom; the preview is faithful; the fallback is neutral). Generate by type, company, one value per batch, up to 10,000, with validity set at creation. Import by pasted serials or ranges; printed numbers remain the codes; imports arrive inactive.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Activation and printing", "12", "<p><b>Activating a complimentary batch.</b></p><p>1. Open the batch from <b>Batches</b> (or from the link shown right after generating or importing).<br>2. Select the cards to issue — <b>select all</b>, or tick individual rows. Partial activation is a normal choice: activate 120 of a 500-card batch for this month's campaign and leave the rest as inert stock.<br>3. Optionally record each card's <b>issued-to</b> and <b>reason</b>. These notes stay on the card's record <b>and are printed on the card itself</b> — a complimentary card that names its recipient and purpose is easy to question if it turns up anywhere else.<br>4. Press <b>Activate</b>. The system <b>records you as the approver, sets the issue and expiry dates, and moves the cards to the Sold (issued) state</b>. Only now can they be printed and redeemed.</p><p>Keep the gate meaningful: activation authority sits with the Voucher Manager, activations should match planned campaigns, and the approver stamp means every activation has a name on it. Chapter 8 includes the check that uses this.</p><p><b>Printing.</b> With a batch open, select the cards and press <b>Print</b>. A print-ready sheet opens in a new tab:</p><p>• <b>Four cards per page</b>, portrait, with dashed cutting guides.<br>• Each card shows the branded panel, the value, a scannable <b>barcode</b> plus the written code, the <b>serial number</b>, and the validity terms. Complimentary cards additionally show who they were issued to, the issue date, and the expiry.</p><p>Print dialog settings — the cause of almost every printing complaint:</p><p>• Pick the correct <b>paper size</b> (A4 or US Letter) in the console's toolbar.<br>• In the browser dialog, keep <b>margins on default (or none)</b> and <b>scale at 100%</b>.</p><p>Cards printing too small, or a blank page after each sheet, are scale-and-margin problems — fix them in the dialog, not the design.</p><p><b>The two printing rules.</b></p><p>• <b>Sellable cards can be printed at any time.</b> Printing them is how the counter is stocked — a printed, unsold sellable card carries no value until a sale binds payment to it, so printing ahead is safe and intended.<br>• <b>Complimentary cards can be printed only after activation.</b> This blocks two problems at once: an unauthorised batch cannot be printed at all, and <b>the same card cannot be printed twice by mistake</b> — two identical printed copies of one live card would be two claims on one balance.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Activate: open batch → select cards (partial is normal) → issued-to and reason (printed on the card) → Activate, which stamps the approver and sets the dates. Print: four per page with cut guides; paper size in the toolbar; margins default, scale 100%. Sellable prints anytime; complimentary only after activation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The counter, the register, and the Overview", "13", "<p><b>Selling at the counter.</b> Two situations, both from module 4's voucher sale, completed here with the console's view:</p><p>• <b>A printed card.</b> The cashier scans the barcode or types the code at payment. The sale marks the card <b>Sold</b> and starts its validity from that day. The customer can be <b>emailed a copy</b> automatically when the sale completes — and if the email never arrives, it can be <b>re-sent from that sale's record</b>.<br>• <b>A blank card</b> (no pre-printed code). The till assigns a <b>fresh code at the moment of payment</b> — common amounts offered, any amount typeable — and prints it on the spot. Cards created this way are <b>grouped into the day's till batch</b>, so at close they can be reconciled: cards issued at this till today against voucher payments taken today. That daily check is on module 8's routine.</p><p>Either way, the code, the value owed and the expiry are recorded identically — the console shows one consistent picture regardless of how the card was born.</p><p><b>Redemption.</b> A presented card pays down the purchase and its balance falls: partly used → <b>Partially redeemed</b>; used up → <b>Redeemed</b>. The profile's partial-redemption and change-policy settings (module 2) govern the arithmetic; the offline policy (Online Only, or Guarded Offline within its caps) governs availability.</p><p><b>The register (Vouchers).</b> Every card, across all batches and companies:</p><p>• <b>Search</b> by code, serial, or the issued-to name. <b>Filter</b> by company, type and state.<br>• The list shows each card's code, type, state, face value, remaining balance, batch and expiry.<br>• Open any card for its <b>detail page</b>: value, balance, key dates, issue details, and a <b>history of every event that changed its balance</b> — the card's own ledger.<br>• From the detail page: <b>reprint</b> that single card (an authorised action — module 4's rule that the slip is handled like cash applies), and <b>cancel (void)</b> for cards lost, damaged or issued in error. Remember Chapter 2: <b>void is permanent, a void card can never be redeemed, and the cancellation stays on the record.</b> Replace a wrongly voided card with a new one; do not argue with the void.</p><p><b>The Overview.</b> The programme at a glance, for one company or all:</p><p>• Total cards and batches.<br>• <b>Outstanding value</b> — what is currently owed to cardholders (the live liability).<br>• <b>Value redeemed</b>, and <b>total face value ever issued</b>.<br>• A breakdown by state where <b>each figure is a link</b> — click through to the matching card list.</p><p>The same figures feed the finance reports: the outstanding-liability statement, activity over a date range, and the lapsed-value summary that Chapter 7 uses.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The accounting and the monthly check", "13", "<p>Four events cover everything a voucher does financially. Learn the four postings and the whole programme's accounting is understood.</p><p><b>Event 1 — a gift card is sold.</b> The cash received is recorded, and the same amount is credited to the <b>liability account</b> — value owed to the cardholder. It is <b>not</b> revenue, and <b>no sales tax is charged</b>.</p><p><b>Event 2 — a card is redeemed against goods.</b> The liability is reduced by the amount spent, and the sale — <b>with its tax</b> — is recognised on the <b>goods the customer takes</b>. Revenue and tax arrive when goods leave, not when the promise was bought.</p><p><b>Event 3 — a complimentary card is redeemed.</b> No money ever came in for this card, so its cost is recognised as a <b>promotional expense</b> at the point of redemption, in the account named in Setup (or the liability account if none was named). The giveaway is costed when it is actually used.</p><p><b>Event 4 — a card expires with value left.</b> The remaining value is no longer owed. A finance manager recognises it as <b>breakage income</b>, clearing the stale liability from the books.</p><p><b>Breakage is a deliberate decision.</b> Three facts about event 4:</p><p>• Recognition is a decision the <b>Accounts Manager</b> makes — <b>the system never books breakage on its own</b>.<br>• The system <b>never records the same lapsed value twice</b> — the sweep can be run safely.<br>• Choose the timing as part of the period routine (for example, sweep expired value quarterly), not ad hoc.</p><p>Why manual: automatic breakage would create income on a schedule nobody reviews, and it would conflict with reality — businesses sometimes honour an expired card as goodwill. Keeping recognition as a dated, named decision keeps the income real and reviewable.</p><p><b>The monthly check.</b> Once a month, compare two numbers:</p><p>1. The programme's <b>outstanding value</b> — from the Overview, or from Gift Voucher Analytics on the till side (which also shows active vouchers, sold and redeemed in the period, and the redemption rate).<br>2. The balance of the <b>liability account</b> in the books.</p><p>They should agree, or the difference should be explainable. While checking, read three more things: the <b>redemption rate</b> (cards coming back is the programme working), the <b>liability trend</b> (December's sales are January's obligations — plan for the redemption wave), and the <b>expired-with-balance pipeline</b> (value awaiting the next breakage decision).</p><p><b>Guarded Offline, revisited.</b> If the profile chose Guarded Offline (module 2), the offline caps — largest single voucher, daily total per terminal — are the one place the register and reality can briefly disagree during an outage. The caps were set as an affordable exposure; the monthly check is where any such disagreement surfaces and is resolved.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — What to watch, and Okelewo's December", "14", "<p>Vouchers are portable value, so the programme carries specific risks. Each has a built-in control and a simple check. Run the checks on the routine given; none takes more than minutes.</p><p><b>1. Unauthorised activation.</b> Risk: a complimentary batch made live outside any planned campaign. Control: the activation gate — every activation carries its approver's name. Check (monthly): read the activation log against the campaign plan; every activated batch should match a known giveaway.</p><p><b>2. Duplicate issue.</b> Risk: two tills issuing the same code, leaving two customers holding one balance. Control: <b>Enforce Voucher Booking</b> (module 2) reserves each code centrally before issue; the printing rule also prevents double-printing complimentary cards. Check: redemption failures — a cluster of already-redeemed errors is the signature of this problem.</p><p><b>3. Buy-back misuse.</b> Risk: the buy-back switch (voucher redeemed for cash) used to pump stored value out of the drawer. Control: leave buy-back <b>off</b> (Okelewo: off permanently); where a business insists on it, require an override. Check (weekly, if enabled): buy-back count and value per till per cashier — the healthy figure is near zero, and any trend needs an explanation.</p><p><b>4. Void after redemption.</b> Risk: value spent, then the card voided to muddy the record. Control: the void is permanent, named, and kept; the card's history shows every balance event in order. Check (monthly): voids by author and by prior state — a void on a partially redeemed card deserves a look.</p><p><b>5. Phantom blank-card sales.</b> Risk: blank cards coded at a till without genuine payment. Control: till-issued cards group into <b>the day's till batch</b>. Check (daily, at close): the till batch against voucher payments taken — cards issued must match money received; any gap is investigated that evening.</p><p><b>6. Complimentary leakage.</b> Risk: giveaway cards drifting to staff, friends and family. Control: issued-to and reason notes, <b>printed on the card</b>; partial activation limited to the campaign's count. Check (monthly): complimentary redemption patterns — repeated names, staff overlaps, one branch out of line.</p><p><b>7. Role overlap.</b> Risk: one person able to create value, sell it, and account for it. Control: the role separation from Chapter 1. Check (at the periodic access review): who holds Voucher Manager, who holds Accounts Manager, and that counter staff hold neither.</p><p><b>Okelewo's December.</b> The launch, in order: <b>Setup</b> first — the accountant wires both accounts, all four validations pass, and the MD's plan to count voucher sales as December revenue is corrected in one meeting with Chapter 3's rule (his conclusion: <i>so it is a loan from customers who intend to spend it here. I can live with that loan</i>). <b>Design</b> by the guided route. <b>Generate</b>: three sellable denominations matching the profile's quick buttons, plus 500 complimentary cards for the season's promotion. <b>Import</b>: leftover 2024 experiment cards brought in by serial range — printed numbers kept as codes, arriving inactive like everything else. <b>Activation</b>: 120 of the 500, issued-to and reason recorded, the approver stamp checked once as a drill. <b>Printing</b>: one sheet wasted learning the scale-at-100% rule, none after. <b>The counter</b>: both selling routes run all month, and the nightly till-batch check reconciles every day. <b>January</b>: the first monthly check matches the Overview's outstanding value to the liability account within rounding; the redemption rate is healthy; the breakage decision for the imported legacy cards is scheduled for their expiry quarter. The accountant's note: <i>it is a small bank — cards out, value owed, books that agree. Which is what it always was; now the records exist.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Review & the seventh law", "11", "<p>Check yourself against each section before the quiz.</p><p><b>The programme (Chapter 1).</b> A voucher is a promise of value — money owed until spent or expired. The console at /vouchers: Overview, Batches, Vouchers, Generate, Import, Design, Setup. Roles: Voucher Manager (runs the console), Administrator, Accounts Manager (accounts and breakage), counter staff (till only). Keep creating, handling and accounting separated.</p><p><b>Kinds, states, the gate (Chapter 2).</b> Sellable: ready at creation, clock from sale. Complimentary: inactive at creation, activated by a manager, clock from activation — so a stolen or premature batch cannot be redeemed. Six states: Available, Sold, Partially redeemed, Redeemed, Expired, Void. Void is permanent and stays on record. Every card keeps a balance and a serial number for life.</p><p><b>Setup (Chapter 3).</b> Liability account (every sale credits it — never revenue, never taxed at sale) and optional promotional expense account. Choose accounts (validated on company, kind, posting, active) or Create defaults for me (accounts, product, tax treatment, payment method in one step). Revenue and tax are recognised at redemption, on the goods. Booking voucher sales as revenue creates four errors at once. Setup before the first batch.</p><p><b>Design and creation (Chapter 4).</b> Branded left half (guided or custom), standard right half. The preview uses the real print engine; no design falls back to a neutral panel. Generate: type, company, one value per batch, up to 10,000, validity at creation. Import: serials pasted or as ranges (0001-0500); printed numbers stay the redemption codes; imports arrive as inactive complimentary batches.</p><p><b>Activation and printing (Chapter 5).</b> Open batch → select (partial is normal) → issued-to and reason (printed on the card) → Activate: approver recorded, dates set, cards move to Sold. Printing: four per page with cut guides; paper size in the toolbar; margins default, scale 100%. Sellable prints anytime (stocking); complimentary only after activation (no unauthorised print, no double print).</p><p><b>Counter and register (Chapter 6).</b> Printed cards scan-to-sell with validity from that day and a re-sendable email copy. Blank cards get a fresh code at payment and join the day's till batch for the close reconciliation. The register finds any card by code, serial or name; the detail page shows the balance history, reprints (authorised), and the permanent void. The Overview's figures link through to card lists.</p><p><b>Accounting (Chapter 7).</b> Four events: sale → liability, no tax; redemption → sale and tax on the goods; complimentary redemption → promotional expense; expiry → breakage income, recognised by a finance manager's deliberate decision — never automatic, never double-counted. Monthly: outstanding value against the liability account, plus the redemption rate, the liability trend, and the expired-value pipeline.</p><p><b>The checks (Chapter 8).</b> Activation log vs campaign plan; already-redeemed clusters; buy-back near zero; voids by author and prior state; the nightly till-batch match; complimentary redemption patterns; the role census.</p><p><b>The seventh law: a voucher is money the business owes, tracked from creation to expiry.</b> Module 8 closes the track: offline, the queue, the daily checks and supervision.</p>"),
]

